<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lola Montez</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1821">1821</a>–<a href="/years/1861">1861</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Eliza Gilbert</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lola Montez was the stage name of Eliza <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilbert</a>, an Irish-born dancer and actress who presented herself as Spanish. In 1846 she became the favourite of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-i-of-bavaria">Ludwig I of Bavaria</a>, who made her Countess of Landsfeld; her influence and arrogance provoked public anger that merged with the revolution of 1848 and forced Ludwig to abdicate. She later performed in America and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lola-montez.jpg" alt="Lola Montez" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antoine Samuel Adam-Salomon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antoine-Samuel_Adam-Salomon_-_Lola_Montez_c1860_-_(MeisterDrucke-148433).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His affair with the dancer <b>Lola Montez</b> discredited him, and he abdicated in March 1848 in favour of his son Maximilian II, who accepted liberal reforms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
